  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nA vertically stacked mid-upper level low, centered over southern \nIdaho early this morning, will slowly track east into Wyoming \nthrough tonight while gradually weakening. This setup will sustain a \ndeep, anomalously moist easterly flow across southwestern Montana \ntoday. Widespread and locally moderate to heavy rain this morning \nwill concentrate along and south of a deformation/convergence axis \nextending eastward across central Montana north of the low. \nConversely, areas north of this axis will experience little to no \nprecipitation. By this afternoon, rainfall will transition to a more \nshowery pattern and pivot back southward over southwestern Montana, \nfollowing the circulation moving into Wyoming.\n\nAs the system continues eastward tonight, a few lingering showers \nremain possible across southwestern Montana into Sunday morning as \nmoisture wraps around its backside. Clearing skies from the west \novernight into Sunday morning may favor fog formation in \nsouthwestern Montana valleys. Upper-level ridging will then rebuild \nacross Montana and the Northern Rockies by Sunday afternoon, \nreturning predominantly sunny, warmer, and drier conditions to the \narea.\n\nA dry weather pattern with above-normal temperatures is expected \nnext week. Broad-scale ridging will attempt to build westward across \nthe CONUS while troughing persists off the West Coast, maintaining \nupper-level heights above average under a southwesterly flow regime. \nHigh temperatures in the upper 70s to around 80 are anticipated, \nrunning roughly 10 degrees above early-autumn normals. \n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nAn area of rainfall with embedded moderate to locally heavy rates \n(0.2 to 0.4 in/hr) will initially focus along the US-12 corridor \nearly this morning. This precipitation is expected to lift northward \ntoward the Little Belt and Snowy mountains later this morning before \ntapering off. The heaviest rainfall accumulations are anticipated \nover and adjacent to the Castle, Big Belt, and Elkhorn mountains, \nthough confidence is somewhat lower regarding how far north higher \namounts will reach into the Little Belts.",
